ch  hasn't been reviewed much should be  about average  so this idea is no it's pretty popular I  see it a lot so we can easily build this  function here called base some and we'll  just dump that into our ratings group  this is our group I object and I will  aggregate by base some and if we look at  what we get out so we still have jaws at  the top but we also see things which  which weren't there before like  Ghostbusters and ghostbusters isn't even  really a horror movie but it was rated  very highly by some of his neighbors and  so it made it in a lot higher up the  list  and there are a lot of ways to do cross  validation I haven't talked a lot about  cross validation yet but I've just  thrown all these parameters that you can  tune and not told you how to tune them  but one way to tune Beijing smoothing is  to look at how your list changes as you  change these parameters if you have a  list of the top 10 movies and you change  the parameters and the list is tech 10  completely different movies then you  probably are you have not quite  converged like a stable set of  parameters but if you change your  Beijing smoothing parameter and the top  10 list of movies doesn't change at all  then it means you're probably pretty  stable and you can like make a little  graph and say I'm I want to wait until  the top 10 movies changes by less than  ten percent with each iteration of the  smoothing parameter or something like  that and use that as I'm kinda makeshift  cross-validation so when you're playing  around with this code on your own some  fun things to do you can look at the  effect of different just different  distance metrics on the recommendations  which I kind of alluded to before you  can also add in the year of the movie  this is something which is also a little  tricky because is it true that a user  who likes movies from 1970 will like  movies from 1971 and what about nineteen  eighty and if it's a continuous variable  does it scale continuously or should  